        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Yazidism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1100 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>, from a pre-Islamic Kurdish religious substratum with deep roots in ancient Iranian beliefs, reorganised in the 12th and 13th centuries by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</a> and his successors at Lalish, in the Shekhan region northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</a> (born in the 1070s, died 1162), the central saint of the faith, regarded as its reformer, renewer or founder; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> venerate him as an earthly incarnation of God. Sheikh Hasan consolidated its institutions in the 1230s and 1240s.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mainly oral: the qewls, sacred hymns in Kurmanji attributed chiefly to Şêx Fexredîn and other disciples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi</a>, with prayers and other poetry; also the mişûr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, the earliest of 1207–1208. The Black Book and Book of Revelation published in 1911 and 1913 are generally held to be forgeries.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">No world total is given: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> estimates range from 70,000 to 500,000; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> has more than 230,000; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> 35,272 (2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> about 18,000 (2002 census), perhaps 6,000 today; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> perhaps 12,000 to 15,000.</span></div>
