    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Yazidism</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazidis.jpg" alt="Yazidis" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Yazidis <span class="kid-tale-cr">Levi Clancy, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Yazidism is the faith of the Yazidis, a Kurdish-speaking people whose homeland is Kurdistan.</p><p>More than 850 years ago, a holy man, Sheikh Adi, settled in the valley of Lalish and united the Yazidi tribes.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Yazidis believe in one God, who gave the world to seven angels led by Tawûsî Melek, the Peacock Angel.</p><p>Their sacred hymns, the qewls, are handed down by word of mouth and sung by their religious leaders.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinjar.jpg" alt="Sinjar" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Sinjar <span class="kid-tale-cr">Amjad.sahep, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Yazidis remember 74 farmans, attacks meant to destroy them, over many centuries.</p><p>In 2014 the Islamic State attacked Sinjar, killed more than 5,000 Yazidis and carried off thousands of women and girls.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Today most Yazidis of the Middle East live in Iraq, and more than 230,000 live in Germany.</p><p>New Yazidi temples opened near Tbilisi in 2015 and in Armenia in 2019.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> believe in one God who created the world and entrusted it to seven angels, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawusi-melek">Tawûsî Melek</a>, the Peacock Angel, whom they hold to be the leader of the archangels and no source of evil.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</a>, a 12th-century mystic who settled at Lalish, united the scattered Yazidi tribes; his tomb is the holiest Yazidi site, and the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of sheikhs, pirs and murids are traced to his reforms.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> remember 74 farmans, campaigns of destruction, from Ottoman expeditions and the massacres of 1832 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> by the Islamic State at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> in 2014, when over 5,000 were killed and thousands of women and girls enslaved.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the Yazidi account of creation, the body of Adam lay lifeless for seven centuries: his soul would not enter it until music, played on the saz and the qudum, came down from heaven.</p></aside>
    </section>

