    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Yazidism, also called Sharfadin, took shape from a pre-Islamic Kurdish religious substratum that was reorganised and given its lasting form by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</a> and his successors in the 12th and 13th centuries. Scholars generally regard it as an independent tradition with deep roots in ancient Iranian beliefs, shaped by later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> influences: some point to parallels with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and the Yaresan tradition, others to the Harranian religion and Hellenistic currents, and the old view of it as an offshoot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> has been widely given up. Before the 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> appear in the sources as Dāsinī, after the region of Dāsin northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. The name Yazidi means “the servant of the creator”; some western scholars derive it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a>, a link all Yazidis reject.</p><p>Sheikh Adi was born in the 1070s at Bait Far in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>, studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> among the leading mystics of his age, Ahmad al-Ghazali among them, and around the age of thirty went to the Hakkari mountains and settled in the valley of Lalish. There he united the scattered Yazidi tribes and returned to them their ancient temple, which members of other faiths had occupied. His disciples spread his teaching that the human spirit may unite directly with God, and Yazidi tradition remembers his time as the “epoch of saints”.</p><p>When he died in 1162 the leadership passed to his nephew Sakhr Abu’l-Barakat. In the 1230s and 1240s Sheikh Hasan, of the same family, brought the consolidation to its height, with a Council of Holy Men and the foundational rules of Yazidi society and religion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yazidis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazidis.jpg" alt="Yazidis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ezidis_celebrating_Ezidi_New_Year_in_April_2018_at_Lalish_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Levi Clancy, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> believe in one God, called Xwedê, Xwedawend, Êzdan or Pedsha, “King”, who has 1,001 names in some hymns and 3,003 in others. Before this world, God made a white pearl from his own light and dwelt in it alone, then created seven angels, the Heft Sirr or “Seven Mysteries”, and gave them all the world’s affairs. Their leader is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawusi-melek">Tawûsî Melek</a>, the Peacock Angel, ruler of the world. Yazidis speak of a divine triad: Tawûsî Melek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi</a> and Sultan Ezid are the three hypostases of the one hidden God, and their identities are sometimes blurred. The pantheon counts 365 holy figures, among them the Xudans, divine powers that control the phenomena of nature.</p><p>Fire, water, air and earth are sacred elements not to be polluted, and Yazidis pray towards the Sun, usually in private. They believe in the rebirth of the soul, described as a change of garment, the “changing of the shirt” (kirasgorîn), and in a cyclic course of time with three purifying disasters, by water, fire and air, of which the first has already happened. This older idea coexists with a younger belief in a collective end of time.</p><p>The New Year, Red Wednesday, falls on the first Wednesday on or after 14 April. Before the Feast of Êzî in December Yazidis fast for three days from sunrise to sunset. The greatest festival is the Feast of the Assembly, from 6 to 13 October, with its pilgrimage to the tomb of Sheikh Adi at Lalish, which Yazidis try to make at least once in their lives.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Yazidi religious literature is mostly poetry, handed down orally, mainly in Kurmanji: hymns, prayers, the Declaration of the Faith and many other genres. Its archaic language can be hard for those not trained in religious knowledge, so prose legends and interpretations of the hymns explain it in the spoken language. The qewls, the sacred hymns, are the most authoritative form. The clergy sing them to set melodies at rites and festivals, and they tell of the creation, the nature of God, ethics, the fate of the soul and the lives of holy figures. In Yazidi belief they came not through prophets but directly from God and from angels in human form; the corpus took its foundational form in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi</a>’s era and is mainly attributed to Şêx Fexredîn and his other disciples.</p><p>The oldest Yazidi documents are the mişûrs, sacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the 13th century; the earliest, the Mišūr of Pir Sini Bahri, dates from 1207–1208. By tradition there are forty, one for each lineage of Pirs, describing its founder and the tribes and priestly lineages tied to him; they are kept in places called stêr. They preserve Sheikh Adi’s own poems: “In the secret of my knowledge there is no God but me.”</p><p>The Book of Revelation and the Black Book, published in 1911 and 1913, are generally regarded as forgeries written by non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> for curious Western travellers and scholars, though their material agrees with Yazidi tradition. The hymns are now being collected with the community’s assent, making Yazidism a scriptural religion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sinjar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinjar.jpg" alt="Sinjar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinjar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amjad.sahep, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi</a>’s following drew people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, the Hakkari mountains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and an Adawi brotherhood named after him is attested across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> into the 15th century, one branch centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, before it dissolved into the Muslim majority. Yazidism was embraced by many Kurdish tribes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a> and was the official religion of principalities such as Sheikhan, Bohtan, Mahmudi, Donboli and Kilis. From the 14th century the Yazidi lands formed seven centres, each with its sinjaq: Lalish and Sheikhan, Shingal (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a>), Hakkari, the lands around Siirt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and Afrin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, and Serhed around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>.</p><p>Persecution and the forced conversion of chiefs reduced Yazidi power from the 15th century, and in the 18th and 19th centuries their numbers declined further. Survivors of the massacres of 1832 fled to Tur Abdin, Mount Judi and Shingal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-wars">Russo-Turkish wars</a> of 1828–1829 and 1877–1878, and the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1915 drove many more from Van, Kars and Bazîd to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>.</p><p>In the 20th century the Yazidi community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> declined steeply, most of its people emigrating to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which now has more than 230,000 Yazidis, from Turkey and more recently <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Refugees have settled in Nebraska since the late 1990s, and since the 1990s many Yazidis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> have left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the West.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Yazidi society is divided into three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, conventionally traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi</a>’s reforms: the sheikhs (şêx), with spiritual and administrative functions; the pirs (pîr), with religious and pastoral functions; and the murids (mirîd), the laypeople. The bond between the castes is held to be like that of siblings, so marriage between them is forbidden, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> marry only Yazidis: those who marry outside are expelled. The system served to hold the community together under pressure from proselytising majorities, and under persecution Sheikh Adi’s followers became a community one can only be born into. The Mîr, prince of the Yazidis, leads it with Baba Sheikh, its religious head, and Sheikh Adi’s family, the Adanî sheikhs, still hold senior offices.</p><p>Every six months the qewals, trained reciters of the hymns, went out from Lalish and Sheikhan under armed protection, leading a sinjaq through the Yazidi villages to uphold the authority of Lalish and the Mîr. Lalish has its own dignitaries, from the Mir Hejj, Prince of the Pilgrimage, to the Baba Chawush, guardian of Sheikh Adi’s sanctuary.</p><p>Children are baptised at birth with water from the White Spring at Lalish, in the rite called mor kirin, “to seal”, and circumcision is not required. Excluded from the status of “People of the Book”, Yazidis had none of the religious rights of Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> under the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">millet system</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> gave them a certain legal status in 1849.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> remember their history as a series of farmans, campaigns of destruction: their songs count 72, and today they speak of 74. In 1246 the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> killed Sheikh Hasan, and in 1414 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Kurdish tribes ransacked Lalish. In 1566 the Ottoman Sheikh al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> allowing the killing of Yazidis and the sale of their women as slaves. An Ottoman army stormed Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> in 1640, killing 3,060 Yazidis. In 1832 the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhammad-kor">Muhammad Pasha of Rawanduz</a> massacred the Yazidis of Sheikhan, enslaving women and children and executing captives who refused Islam. In 1892 an Ottoman campaign killed around 500 in Sinjar and made Lalish a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> until 1904, and many Yazidis died in the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> the Ba’athist government destroyed Yazidi villages and moved their people by force, and in 2007 bombings at Qahtaniya killed hundreds. In August 2014 the Islamic State, which had declared the Yazidis devil worshippers, captured Sinjar after the Peshmerga withdrew, beginning the genocide of the Yazidis: over 5,000 were killed, thousands of women and girls were forced into sexual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and more than 500,000 fled. Tens of thousands trapped on Mount Sinjar escaped when Kurdish fighters opened a corridor. Nadia Murad, enslaved in 2014, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2018.</p><p>Most Yazidis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> live in Iraq’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> and Duhok <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>; new temples opened near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 2015 and at Aknalich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 2019.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Yazidism is the ethnic religion of the Kurdish-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a>, rooted in a pre-Islamic Kurdish and Iranian heritage and given its form by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-adi-ibn-musafir">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir</a>, whose tomb at Lalish is its holiest site. Yazidis venerate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawusi-melek">Tawûsî Melek</a>, the Peacock Angel, and keep their faith in hymns and endogamous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>. Long persecuted as “devil-worshippers”, they suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> by the Islamic State at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> in 2014.</em></p>

